          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which nerve is most at risk of injury during ligation of the inferior thyroid artery?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>External laryngeal nerve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The external laryngeal nerve runs alongside the superior thyroid artery, not the inferior thyroid artery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Phrenic nerve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The phrenic nerve descends on the anterior surface of scalenus anterior and is not in the operative field of thyroid surgery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Recurrent laryngeal nerve</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The recurrent laryngeal nerve crosses near the inferior thyroid artery and drives all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypoglossal nerve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The hypoglossal nerve runs in the submandibular region and is not encountered during thyroid surgery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the nerve–artery pairs: the inferior thyroid artery is closely related to the recurrent laryngeal nerve, while the superior thyroid artery travels with the external laryngeal nerve."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The recurrent laryngeal nerve is closely related to the inferior thyroid artery, making it vulnerable during thyroid surgery. Unilateral injury causes hoarseness; bilateral injury threatens the airway."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
