          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>After total thyroidectomy, a patient develops a weak voice that tires easily but</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>After total thyroidectomy, a patient develops a weak voice that tires easily but is not frankly hoarse. Which structure was most likely injured?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Recurrent laryngeal nerve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury produces frank hoarseness or, if bilateral, airway compromise — not the subtle voice fatigue described here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Parathyroid glands</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Parathyroid damage causes a dangerous fall in blood calcium (hypoparathyroidism), not voice changes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Thoracic duct</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The thoracic duct is at a higher level on the left side of the neck and is not typically at risk during thyroidectomy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>External laryngeal nerve</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The external laryngeal nerve supplies the cricothyroid muscle, which tenses the vocal cord. Its injury produces a characteristically weak, easily tired voice.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A weak, easily tired voice — distinct from frank hoarseness — points to external laryngeal nerve injury. This nerve accompanies the superior thyroid artery and innervates the cricothyroid muscle, which tenses the vocal cord."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The external laryngeal nerve runs alongside the superior thyroid artery and innervates the cricothyroid muscle. Distinguishing its injury (voice fatigue) from recurrent laryngeal nerve damage (frank hoarseness) is essential in post-thyroidectomy assessment."
                }
              ],
